menu "APRS heap instrumentation"

    config APRS_HEAP_REPORT
        bool "Log a periodic heap line"
        default y
        help
            Emit one line per period, from the APRS service's 1 Hz tick, with
            the free heap, the largest free block and the all-time minimum
            free heap.

            These three figures are what describe the allocator on the healthy
            path. Every other heap number this firmware logs is printed after
            something has already failed, which says what the heap looked like
            once the damage was done and nothing about how it got there. A
            watermark that fell overnight leaves no trace without this line.

            It costs three allocator queries per period and no memory, so it
            is meant to stay on in a normal build.

    config APRS_HEAP_REPORT_PERIOD_S
        int "Seconds between heap lines"
        depends on APRS_HEAP_REPORT
        range 10 3600
        default 60
        help
            One line a minute is enough to place a slow drift or a sudden
            notch on the clock, and short enough that a minute-by-minute trace
            lines up with the individual events that caused it.

            A shorter period only adds resolution up to the rate at which the
            heap actually moves; below about ten seconds the line mostly
            records the noise of the network stack's own buffers.

    config APRS_HEAP_REPORT_TASKS
        bool "Add the per-task usage table to each heap line"
        depends on APRS_HEAP_REPORT && HEAP_TASK_TRACKING
        default y
        help
            Follow each heap line with the per-task overview table that the
            heap component builds when task tracking is enabled, attributing
            the memory currently held to the task that allocated it.

            This is the tool that turns "the free heap keeps falling" into a
            task name in a single dump. It is only offered when
            CONFIG_HEAP_TASK_TRACKING is selected, which is deliberately not
            the default: tracking costs RAM per live allocation and slows
            every allocation and free down, so it belongs in a diagnostic
            build and should be turned off again afterwards.

    config APRS_HEAP_INTEGRITY_CHECK
        bool "Run a periodic heap integrity check"
        default n
        help
            Sweep every heap on a slow timer and log the result, so that
            corruption is ruled out rather than chased as a leak. Corrupted
            allocator structures present as inexplicable heap behaviour: free
            counts that move without an obvious owner, allocations that fail
            with memory apparently available, crashes far from the code that
            caused them.

            The sweep holds each heap's lock while it walks it, so every other
            task blocks if it tries to allocate meanwhile. That is why this is
            off by default and on a slow timer when on.

            What the sweep can see depends on the heap corruption detection
            level. With the default (no poisoning) it only checks the
            allocator's own structures; select "Light impact" or
            "Comprehensive" under "Heap memory debugging" to also verify the
            canary bytes around every allocated block.

    config APRS_HEAP_INTEGRITY_PERIOD_S
        int "Seconds between heap integrity checks"
        depends on APRS_HEAP_INTEGRITY_CHECK
        range 10 3600
        default 300
        help
            The sweep is long enough to be worth spacing out. Five minutes
            still brackets corruption to a window small enough to correlate
            with the rest of the log, at a cost the station does not feel.

endmenu
